<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uluj Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1587">1587</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Occhiali, Kılıç Ali Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uluj Ali was an Italian-born Ottoman corsair and admiral, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> as Giovanni Dionigi Galeni and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs as a young man. Converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, he rose to be beylerbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> from 1568. At Lepanto in 1571 he commanded the Ottoman left wing and escaped with much of his squadron; as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapudan-pasha">Kapudan Pasha</a> he then rebuilt the fleet and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1574.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uluj-ali.jpg" alt="Uluj Ali" width="286" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Leunclavius, 1586 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulu%C3%A7_Al%C3%AC,_Occhiali,_Uccialli_(Giovan_Dionigi_Galeni,_alias_Ali_Kilic_Pasha,_1519-1587),_immagine_in_Johannes_Leunclavius_(Lewenklau,_1533-41-1595)_Wasser-_und_Speisentr%C3%A4ger_(1586),_Oesterreichische_Nationalbibliothek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
